{
  "assessment": "The source provides a universal framework for the time evolution of holographic and quantum complexity, with explicit formulas for the generating function and complexity in the GUE example. The main quantitative cluster is the codimension-one complexity time evolution, with two regimes (t < T_H and t >= T_H) controlled by time t and parameters M, T_H. The source reports the central formula C(t) = M t (1 - t/T_H + t^2/(3 T_H^2)) for t < T_H and C(t) = (1/3) M T_H for t >= T_H, with an undetermined additive constant. The pole structure and level repulsion are identified as necessary and sufficient conditions. The extraction is case_authoring_ready because there is a numeric control (time t), a numeric readout (complexity C), a quantitative mechanism (the pole structure and sine kernel), and an explicit domain (t < T_H and t >= T_H).",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Masamichi Miyaji, Universal Time Evolution of Holographic and Quantum Complexity, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/fsdt-d3p9",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/fsdt-d3p9",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2507.23667v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper introduces generating functions for holographic and quantum complexity measures, showing universal slope-ramp-plateau time evolution. It derives explicit time evolution for codimension-one complexity in the GUE example, with linear growth and late-time plateau, and extends to codimension-zero observables. The extraction focuses on the codimension-one complexity time evolution formula.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "pole_structure_mechanism",
      "statement": "The matrix element of the generating function has a first-order pole at E_ij = -i tilde_alpha, with tilde_alpha = M alpha + O(alpha^2). This pole structure is necessary and sufficient for linear growth of holographic complexity.",
      "equations": [
        "langle E_i | e^{-alpha C} | E_j rangle ~ (tilde_alpha / (pi e^{S0} D(E))) * 1/((tilde_alpha + i E_ij)(tilde_alpha - i E_ij))",
        "tilde_alpha = M alpha + O(alpha^2)"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["E_i", "E_j", "E_ij", "tilde_alpha", "M", "alpha", "C", "S0", "D(E)"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "M_linear_growth_rate",
          "value": 1.0,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Linear growth rate of complexity; coefficient in tilde_alpha = M alpha",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 297,
              "line_end": 297,
              "support": "The classical linear growth rate M is determined by the location of the pole, E_ij = ± i M alpha."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 600,
              "line_end": 601,
              "support": "tilde_alpha = M alpha + O(alpha^2)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 242,
          "line_end": 248,
          "support": "Linear growth occurs if and only if the matrix elements develop a pole structure: <E_i|e^{-alpha C}|E_j> ~ (tilde_alpha/(e^{S0} pi D(E))) * 1/((tilde_alpha + i E_ij)(tilde_alpha - i E_ij)), with tilde_alpha = M alpha + O(alpha^2)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 364,
          "line_end": 368,
          "support": "A first-order pole with lim_{alpha->0} E_R(alpha)=0 and lim_{alpha->0} E_I(alpha) ~ -M alpha is necessary for linear growth; no other pole type produces linear growth."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 595,
          "line_end": 601,
          "support": "Universal pole structure: <E_i|e^{-alpha C}|E_j> ~ 1/((tilde_alpha + i E_ij)(tilde_alpha - i E_ij)), with tilde_alpha = M alpha + O(alpha^2)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "sine_kernel_mechanism",
      "statement": "The ensemble-averaged two-point spectral correlator for GUE is given by the sine kernel, which exhibits level repulsion and leads to the ramp and plateau in the generating function.",
      "equations": [
        "overline{<D(E_i)D(E_j)>} approx Dbar(E_i)Dbar(E_j) - sin^2(e^{S0} pi E_ij Dbar(E))/(e^{S0} pi E_ij)^2"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["D(E_i)", "D(E_j)", "E_ij", "Dbar(E)", "S0"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 267,
          "line_end": 273,
          "support": "GUE two-point spectral correlator: overline{<D(E_i)D(E_j)>} approx Dbar(E_i)Dbar(E_j) - sin^2(e^{S0} pi E_ij Dbar(E))/(e^{S0} pi E_ij)^2."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 551,
          "line_end": 557,
          "support": "Spectral two-point correlation function for GUE with sine kernel and contact term."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "microcanonical_ensemble",
      "statement": "Microcanonical ensemble with energy window centered at E0, width Delta E, and rescaled density of states D(E).",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 184,
          "line_end": 188,
          "support": "Microcanonical ensemble with finite energy window centered around E0, E_i in [E0 - Delta E/2, E0 + Delta E/2]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "gue_universality",
      "statement": "Spectral correlations follow GUE universality for |E_i - E_j| << E_Th (Thouless energy).",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 266,
          "line_end": 266,
          "support": "For |E_i - E_j| << E_Th, the ensemble-averaged spectral correlator coincides with that of the appropriate Wigner-Dyson ensemble, e.g., GUE."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "alpha_T_H_large",
      "statement": "The regulator alpha satisfies alpha T_H >> 1 for the slope-ramp-plateau formula.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 276,
          "line_end": 276,
          "support": "For a generic value of the regulator alpha satisfying alpha T_H >> 1, the time evolution of the generating function is dominated by the following contribution."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
